#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115050 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b41a05-7b11-52b8-a407-af505dffaf10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Data Set Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "e148a574-9aa3-57f1-a186-4fbd543db3e7" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7638" :question/problem "What is the variance of the following data set?\n$5, \\: 13, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 3$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance of the data set is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot (5 + 13 + 6 + 8 + 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}[(5 - 7)^{2} + (13 - 7)^{2} + (6 - 7)^{2} + (8 - 7)^{2} + (3 - 7)^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}[4 + 36 + 1 + 1 + 16] \\\\\n&= \\frac{58}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115051 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0040fc1-2134-5eaa-a3e8-6c7a457f75f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Data Set Using the Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "83414114-1b1d-5500-9a38-648a90d0244c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7639" :question/problem "What is the variance of the following data set?\n$4, \\: 5, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 8, \\: 11, \\: 3$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}$. There are $n=7$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot (4 + 5 + 4 + 7 + 8 + 11 + 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot 42 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}$. Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot (4^{2} + 5^{2} + 4^{2} + 7^{2} + 8^{2} + 11^{2} + 3^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot (16 + 25 + 16 + 49 + 64 + 121 + 9) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= \\frac{300}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{300}{7} - (6)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{300}{7} - 36 \\\\\n&= \\frac{300 - 252}{7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{48}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115052 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a1d37026-f331-5e80-8640-00289af07606" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7640" :question/problem "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n$7, \\: 5, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 1, \\: 3$" :question/worked-solution "The standard deviation of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2} = \\sqrt{\\,\\,\\, \\vphantom{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n} \\overline{x^2} - \\left(\\overline{x} \\right)^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}$. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (7 + 5 + 0 + 2 + 1 + 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 18 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}$. Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (7^{2} + 5^{2} + 0^{2} + 2^{2} + 1^{2} + 3^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (49 + 25 + 0 + 4 + 1 + 9) \\\\\n&= \\frac{88}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{3} - (3)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{3} - 9 \\\\\n&= \\frac{44 - 27}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{17}{3}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115053 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eab4249-544e-56c6-81c0-e6db84033701" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance Given the Number, Sum, and Sum of Squares of the Data Points"}] :question/id #uuid "234b1f04-e423-5d6f-8084-15b55d0632ca" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7641" :question/problem "Compute the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a data set $x_i$ consisting of $8$ data points, where\n\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320$." :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance of the data set is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}$. There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40\n$$\n\nso we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}∑_(i = 1)^(8)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}$. We know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320\n$$\n\nso we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}∑_(i = 1)^(8)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 320 \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= 40 - (5)^{2} \\\\\n&= 40 - 25 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6109e8da-0ddf-5915-8253-a0fb57f138a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{716/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6109e8da-0ddf-5915-8253-a0fb57f138a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{716/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232799} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3b2d4f-dd88-551d-938f-f7afeff7970e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112334}] :question/id #uuid "5710fea8-dfda-5618-84dc-c9d9011e12ae" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-246726" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nCompute the standard deviation $σ$ of the following data set:\n$-9,-7, 1, 3, 4$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n$σ =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"why-we-use-the-squared-difference\"></a>" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
